        # ---------------------------------------------------------------- decision trees: how a question is chosen
        dict(kind='idea', title='A decision tree settles the umbrella question with two yes-or-no questions',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_umbrella'),
             lead='Twelve past days built this tree. A new day starts at the top.',
             points=['Follow the answers down to a leaf: cloudy and over 50%, so carry it.', 'The questions are splits. The answer boxes are leaves.', 'The longest path asks 2 questions: the depth is 2.'],
             notes='The first method is the decision tree, and you already use one when you decide about an umbrella. Is the sky cloudy? If not, leave it. If so, is the forecast over 50 per cent rain? If not, leave it, and if so, carry it. A decision tree is a model made of yes-or-no questions about features. Each question is a split, each answer box is a leaf, and the number of questions along the longest path is the depth, here 2. I drew this tree from twelve invented past days: in the leaf on the right, 4 of 5 days rained. A new day follows the answers down until it reaches a leaf. But why ask about the sky first? To answer that, we need a way to say how mixed a group of days is.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='How mixed is a group? Start with the share p of each answer',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_shares'),
             lead='A good question leaves groups in which the days agree.',
             points=['The share p of an answer is its count divided by the group size.', 'All 12 days: 4 rained, so p rain = 4 ÷ 12 = 1/3, and p dry = 2/3.', 'A pure group has one answer, with a share of 1.'],
             notes='To choose a question, the tree needs to know how mixed a group is. Look at three groups of days. Five clear days are all dry: that group is pure, every day gives the same answer. Four days, two rainy and two dry, are as mixed as two answers can be. Our twelve days sit in between. The tool for all of this is the share, written p. The share of an answer is how many days gave it, divided by how many days are in the group. Four rainy days out of twelve is a share of 4 divided by 12, one third. The dry share is eight twelfths, two thirds. The shares always add up to 1. Gini impurity, next, turns those shares into one number for the whole group.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Gini impurity is the chance that two days picked at random disagree',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_gini_steps'),
             lead='Pick two days at random. How likely are they to disagree?',
             points=['Both rainy: 1/3 × 1/3 = 1/9.', 'G = 1 − Σ pₖ²: pₖ is the share of answer k; Σ means add up, one term per answer.', '0 is pure; 0.5 is half and half.'],
             notes='The standard measure is Gini impurity, and we build it on the twelve days. Pick two days at random, putting the first back before you pick the second. The chance both were rainy is one third times one third, a ninth. So a share is squared because p times p is the chance that both picks give that answer. Both dry: two thirds times two thirds, four ninths. Add the squares: five ninths is the chance the two days agree. Gini is 1 minus that, four ninths, 0.44, the chance they disagree. The formula says the same: G equals 1 minus the sum of the squared shares. The Greek letter sigma means add up, one term for each answer. A pure group scores 0 and half and half scores 0.5, so lower means purer.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Asking about the sky first lowers the Gini impurity more than asking about the forecast',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_two_questions'),
             lead='Score each possible first question on the same 12 days, then keep the best.',
             points=['Sky: 7/12 × 0.49 + 5/12 × 0 = 0.29, a drop of 0.16.', 'Forecast: 8/12 × 0.50 + 4/12 × 0 = 0.33, a drop of 0.11.', 'The sky question drops more, so the tree asks it first.'],
             notes='With a measure in hand, we can score a question. The twelve days start at a Gini of 0.44. Ask whether the sky is cloudy. The seven cloudy days, four rainy and three dry, score 0.49; the five clear days are all dry, so 0. Why weight by size? A big mixed group matters more than a small pure one, so seven twelfths of 0.49 plus five twelfths of 0 gives 0.29. The drop, about 0.16, is the Gini gain. Now the forecast. On eight days it said over 50 per cent, and four of those rained: Gini 0.5. The other four stayed dry: 0. Eight twelfths of 0.5 is 0.33, a drop of only 0.11. The sky wins, so the tree asks it first. The computer scores every question this way, keeps the largest drop, then repeats inside each group.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Finding one card among eight takes three yes-or-no questions, so log₂ 8 = 3',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_log2'),
             lead='A second measure, entropy, counts questions. First the counting tool.',
             points=['Each question halves the choices: 8, 4, 2, 1.', 'log₂ n: halvings from n down to 1. log₂ 8 = 3.', 'In between: log₂ 3 = 1.585.'],
             notes='Gini is one way to measure a mixed group. The other common way is entropy, and it rests on one small tool, so let me build that first. Suppose a card is hidden among eight and you may only ask yes-or-no questions. Ask, is it in the left half? Whatever the answer, four remain. Ask again: two. Once more: one. Three questions for eight equal choices. The maths name for this count is the logarithm to base 2, written log 2. Log 2 of 8 is 3, of 4 is 2, of 2 is 1. For numbers in between, the answer falls in between: log 2 of 3 is about 1.585, an average over many rounds. A calculator or spreadsheet gives it.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Entropy counts the yes-or-no questions needed, on average, to learn the answer',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_entropy_steps'),
             lead='For the 12 days, the entropy is 0.918 questions.',
             points=['Rain is 1 day in 3: log₂ 3 = 1.585 questions. Dry is 1 in 1.5: 0.585.', 'Weight each by its share: 1/3 × 1.585 + 2/3 × 0.585 = 0.918.', 'H = Σ p × log₂(1/p), often written −Σ p log₂ p. 0 is pure; 1 is half and half.'],
             notes='Now entropy itself, on the same twelve days. Entropy measures how many yes-or-no questions you need, on average, to learn a day’s answer. A rare answer costs more questions. Rain happened one day in three, so it costs log 2 of 3, 1.585 questions. Dry happened one day in one and a half, which costs log 2 of 1.5, 0.585. Now weight each by its share, as in an average: one third of 1.585 is 0.528, two thirds of 0.585 is 0.390. Add them: 0.918 questions. That is the formula: entropy H is the sum, over the answers, of the share times log 2 of one over the share. Books write the same thing with a minus sign. A pure group needs no questions; half and half needs one.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Scored by entropy, the sky question gains 0.34 and still comes first',
             visual=dict(kind='t02_split_measure', measure='entropy'),
             lead='The same split, now scored by entropy.',
             points=['Cloudy: 4/7 × log₂(7/4) + 3/7 × log₂(7/3) = 0.985. Clear: 0.', 'Weighted: 7/12 × 0.985 + 5/12 × 0 = 0.575.', 'Information gain: 0.918 − 0.575 = 0.343. Gini and entropy pick the same question here.'],
             notes='We score the sky question again, this time with entropy. The twelve days start at 0.918. The cloudy group, four rainy and three dry out of seven, has an entropy of 0.985: very mixed, close to the maximum of 1. The clear group is pure, so 0. Weight by size as before: seven twelfths of 0.985, plus nothing, gives 0.575. The drop, 0.918 minus 0.575, is 0.343. This drop in entropy is called information gain: how much uncertainty, counted in questions, the split removed. The forecast question gains only 0.25 by the same sum, so the tree keeps the sky, the question with the largest gain. Gini and entropy usually agree, and they do here. The tool in our demonstration uses Gini, its default setting, on business data.'),
